{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C5JLTWt7jmyQa6g5ZCCwdD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — irt_personfit_contam\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (用户确认；ARR submission DDL 5/25 — 需确认可行性)\n5\t- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (abstract 7/21, full paper 7/28, ~10 weeks)\n6\t- Backup: ICLR 2027 (~Sep 2026, ~17 weeks)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n9\t\n10\t**Claim 1 (Minimal-Information Detection Framework + Controlled Validation)**:\n11\tIRT person-fit statistics provide a principled contamination detection framework at the minimal-information operating point — requiring only binary response patterns. Simulation establishes high theoretical detection power (97.9% on MMLU-scale benchmarks), and bootstrap null distributions are well-calibrated for large benchmarks (MMLU/HellaSwag KS reject 8.3%, but overall 40% due to small benchmarks like HumanEval 75%, ARC-C 58.3% — calibration degrades with n_items). In simulation, lz* matches or outperforms supervised binary classifiers (LR/RF with oracle labels) at low contamination (5-10%), leveraging IRT structure as inductive bias. Controlled contamination experiments (QLoRA SFT injection on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) validate the framework across model families: Qwen at 15% contamination yields |Cohen's d|=3.42 (n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected) with clear seen/unseen memorization fingerprint; Llama confirms cross-family detection with |Cohen's d|=2.95 at 15% and 3.31 at 50% (n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected), both large effects. θ-fixed analysis reveals a θ-shift boundary condition: when θ is re-estimated on contaminated data, detection sensitivity can degrade (model-dependent; Qwen shows free-θ non-monotonicity due to high baseline misfit, Llama does not), but fixing θ to pre-contamination baseline restores monotonic dose-response in both families. The memorization fraction (~35-38% at 15% contamination, consistent across families) quantifies the SFT generalization effect.\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 2 (Detection Characterization)**:\n14\tDetection power increases with benchmark size (n_items) and varies with contamination level, following an empirical power function (R²=0.65) that provides approximate practical guidance for practitioners assessing detectability on benchmarks of known size — but should NOT be described as \"predictive theory\" or \"scaling law.\" Two distinct signal regimes: partial contamination produces aberrant patterns (lz* << 0), full contamination produces over-consistent patterns (lz* >> 0). For sufficiently large benchmarks (≥10K items), theoretical power exceeds 90% at 15% contamination. Note: power function gives an **upper bound** (assumes all contaminated items produce aberrant signal); SFT generalization reduces effective signal — ctrl-contam measures actual memorization fraction to derive adjusted (\"expected\") power. Both upper bound and adjusted power should be reported. **Baseline comparison (exp-baseline-comparison)**: At model level, lz* achieves 8× the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman/U3) on identical data (|d|=3.42 vs |d|=0.43 for Qwen 15%), demonstrating the value of full 4PL IRT parameterization. At item level, ALL binary-only methods achieve near-random discrimination (AUROC 0.52–0.63, including oracle supervised classifiers), revealing contamination produces a diffuse statistical signature detectable only through model-level aggregation over thousands of items. Accuracy z-score trivially detects in controlled settings (z=160 due to near-zero accuracy variance) but lacks specificity in real-world scenarios. The IRT framework enables principled aggregation by calibrating each item's expected response probability.\n15\t\n16\t**Claim 3 (Empirical Findings from Real LLM Data)**:\n17\tThree empirical findings from applying IRT person-fit to real LLM response data: (a) **Universal 4PL model misfit** — nearly all (model, benchmark) cells produce extreme lz* values (|lz*| >> 5, 57/60 cells flagged after BH-FDR correction), revealing that LLM response patterns fundamentally differ from classical test-taker patterns. This is a genuine property of LLM responses (bootstrap null is well-calibrated), not a statistical artifact. The simulation-to-reality gap motivates the Δlz* design. (b) **PSN-IRT 12-model case study** — detailed anomaly profiles with validated 4PL parameters (Gemini-1.5: 100% MMLU accuracy, lz*=+31.81, θ=0.78; selective vs uniform misfit patterns across benchmarks; Ward clustering reveals contamination typology). (c) **IRT parameter estimation challenges** — MML-EM refit from v1 leaderboard data (N=63→178, covering all available models) yields b_r=0.66 (partial difficulty validation) but a_r≈0.13 (discrimination failure, well below 0.2 threshold), quantifying the sample size gap between classical IRT and neural IRT estimation in LLM contexts. 13K+ model per-item data is publicly available for future large-scale audits once estimation challenges are resolved.\n18\t\n19\t## Story Spine\n20\t\n21\t**Problem**: Benchmark contamination undermines LLM evaluation reliability. As benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow, contamination risk increases, but all existing detection methods require privileged access — training data (n-gram overlap), token probabilities (Min-K%, MIA), model weights (internal probing), or specially crafted prompts (Time Travel). A contaminated LLM is functionally analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge, yet no work has connected the mature psychometric literature on cheating detection to LLM benchmark integrity.\n22\t\n23\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods circa 2026 span five families: time-based partition, MIA/Min-K%++ (ICLR 2024), Time Travel probing (ICLR 2024 Spotlight), ConStat performance-based testing (NeurIPS 2024), and CoDeC in-context learning effects (ICLR 2026). Wang et al. (2025) show all MIA-based methods fail under post-RL contamination. Critically, ALL require more than binary response patterns — no method operates at the minimal-information level.\n24\t\n25\t**Our framing**: We establish the minimal-information operating point on the contamination detection frontier. By repurposing IRT person-fit statistics — developed over decades for detecting exam cheating — we ask: \"What can be detected from binary response patterns alone?\" We show: (1) in simulation, person-fit statistics achieve high detection power (97.9% on MMLU-scale benchmarks) with well-calibrated null distributions; (2) in real LLM data, response patterns universally deviate from 4PL predictions — the statistical framework is sound, but LLMs genuinely violate IRT assumptions (local independence, unidimensionality), producing extreme lz* values across nearly all models; (3) controlled contamination experiments leverage this by measuring Δlz* between clean and contaminated models — since the null is well-calibrated, within-model shifts are statistically interpretable. The universal model misfit finding is itself a contribution — it reveals that LLM response patterns are fundamentally different from classical test-taker patterns.\n26\t\n27\t**Headline result template**: \"Using only binary response patterns from the target model (with externally calibrated IRT item parameters), IRT-FIT detects controlled contamination with |Cohen's d|>2.9 across two model families (Qwen, Llama), achieving 8× the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman/U3). The HT statistic (requiring only item difficulty) achieves comparable model-level detection power — the IRT person-fit family collectively dominates simpler indices, with ℓz*'s advantage lying in mechanistic interpretability (θ-shift analysis, memorization fraction quantification) and [pending: post-RL robustness vs HT]. Model-level aggregation over thousands of items yields robust detection where all item-level methods are near-random (AUROC 0.52–0.63). The IRT framework provides unique mechanistic insight: θ-shift analysis reveals how contamination raises ability estimates, and memorization fraction (35–38%) bridges theoretical power (97.9%) and observed detection capacity.\"\n28\t\n29\t## Venue Fit Risks\n30\t\n31\t- **EMNLP**: NLP evaluation methodology fits well. Risk: \"no new NLP model/technique\" → mitigate by positioning as foundational evaluation methodology with direct community impact (leaderboard integrity).\n32\t- **AAAI**: PSN-IRT was AAAI 2026 Oral — strong psychometrics+AI audience. Risk: less NLP-specific reviewer pool.\n33\t- **Cross-discipline concern**: IRT/psychometrics may be unfamiliar to ML reviewers → need self-contained exposition in Section 3 (IRT background), avoid jargon, provide intuitive explanations.\n34\t\n35\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n36\t\n37\t| # | Concern | Response Strategy | Supporting Experiment |\n38\t|---|---------|------------------|----------------------|\n39\t| R1 | \"Just applying existing IRT to LLMs\" | Frame as establishing new operating point, not cross-domain transfer. Person-fit for contamination is unexplored. Downgrade \"cross-domain\" from contribution to background. | — |\n40\t| R2 | \"No ground truth contamination labels\" | Controlled contamination experiment (fine-tune clean models on benchmark items). Phase B anomalies are converging evidence, not proof. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n41\t| R3 | \"Power too low for small benchmarks\" | Present power function analysis as contribution, not limitation. Derive theoretical relationship. | exp-power-theory |\n42\t| R4 | \"Only 12 models\" | 12 models with validated 4PL parameters provide detailed anomaly profiles. Universal misfit (57/60 flagged) spans sufficient models to establish the pattern. 13K+ v1 per-item data availability discussed as future work. Ctrl-contam covers 4-5 model families. | exp-ctrl-contam, phase_b_personfit |\n43\t| R5 | \"SFT generalization smooths signal\" | Controlled experiment includes domain-level analysis. Discuss hard-item contamination resistance. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n44\t| R6 | \"Absolute lz* thresholds don't work on real data\" | Bootstrap null is well-calibrated (KS pass for large benchmarks); the issue is LLMs genuinely deviate from 4PL. Universal model misfit is a finding, not an artifact. Δlz* leverages the well-calibrated null for within-model comparisons. | exp-ctrl-contam, exp-empirical-null |\n45\t| R7 | \"Post-RL contamination?\" | Hypothesis test: GRPO after SFT contamination on Qwen 3.5-9B. | exp-post-rl |\n46\t| R8 | \"4PL vs 3PL — does d parameter matter?\" | Ablation comparing 4PL vs 3PL(d=1) detection sensitivity. | exp-4pl-ablation |\n47\t| R9 | \"No same-level binary-only baselines\" | **Completed (exp-baseline-comparison)**: Model-level: lz* |d|=3.42 vs Guttman/U3 |d|=0.43 (8× gap) — full IRT parameterization far exceeds difficulty-only person-fit. **HT is nearly as powerful as ℓz* (avg |d| comparable)** — the \"8×\" headline must include HT comparison to avoid selective framing. Item-level: all methods near-random (AUROC 0.52–0.63). acc z-score detects trivially (z=160) but lacks specificity. | exp-baseline-comparison |\n48\t| R12 | \"HT ≈ ℓz* — why use ℓz*?\" | **[Dream D2 三档一致]** HT achieves comparable |d| with weaker requirements (difficulty only, no 4PL). **四场景应对 (D022, Reviewer validated)**：(Best) HT post-RL 丢失 + ℓz* 保留 → headline 差异化；(Good) HT 弱化 + ℓz* 更强 → quantitative comparison；(Neutral) 均保留 → 转用数据无关差异化（见下）；(Bad) 均丢失 → negative-as-contribution, scope SFT。**数据无关差异化（任何场景均可用）**：(i) ℓz* 有参数化 null (≈N(0,1), bootstrap 已验证) → 可直接假设检验, HT 无此性质；(ii) Snijders 校正补偿 θ 估计误差 (Var*=Var_l - C²/I(θ)), HT 无此校正；(iii) ℓz* 用 a/b/c/d 四参数加权 item 贡献, HT 仅用 b → MMLU 跨 57 科目 item 异质性大时 ℓz* 理论更精确。这些点不依赖 post-RL 结果，论文中应明确讨论。 | exp-baseline-comparison, exp-post-rl |\n49\t| R13 | \"Minimal-information overstates\" | **[Dream D2 三档一致]** \"Minimal-information operating point\" obscures PSN-IRT 4PL parameter dependency. Reframe to \"minimal information **from the target model**\" or \"minimal runtime information\" — explicitly acknowledge PSN-IRT calibration as an external input requirement. | — |\n50\t| R10 | \"Leaderboard audit baseline unreliable\" | Leaderboard audit abandoned (D011). ctrl-contam is the validated detection. 12-model case study uses PSN-IRT validated parameters directly (no baseline estimation needed). v1 data availability noted as future work. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n51\t| R11 | \"Power function is upper bound\" | Report both theoretical upper bound AND adjusted power (memorization fraction from ctrl-contam seen/unseen split). Gap = SFT generalization effect quantification. | exp-ctrl-contam, exp-power-theory |\n52\t\n53\t## Key Narrative Elements (from External Review + Distillation)\n54\t\n55\t1. **Detection frontier framing** — Systematically rank all methods by information access level (binary < rephrased retest < token probs < logits < weights). IRT-FIT defines the bottom of this frontier.\n56\t2. **Definitional pivot (à la ConStat)** — \"All prior contamination detection methods require privileged access. We ask a more fundamental question: what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?\" This reframes our work as problem reformulation, not just cross-domain transfer.\n57\t3. **RL-robustness as structural advantage** — Wang et al. (ICLR 2026) proves all 10 log-prob methods ≈ random on RL-trained reasoning models. Person-fit operates on binary response patterns, not token distributions — structurally immune to RL-induced distributional shifts. This is our strongest motivation against log-prob baselines.\n58\t4. **Scalability potential (deferred to future work)** — 13K+ v1 per-item data publicly available. IRT-FIT is uniquely suited for retrospective audit (only needs archival binary data). BUT: current paper does NOT include large-scale audit (D011: IRT refit failed). Mention data availability + roadmap in Discussion/Future Work. NEVER claim \"leaderboard audit\" or \"large-scale detection\" in current paper.\n59\t5. **Inverted-U as empirical characterization** — Derive from: (a) contamination→0%: signal drowned in sampling noise; (b) contamination→100%: identifiability collapse. Simulation fit R²=0.957; real-data fit R²=0.65 (gap due to SFT generalization). Frame as \"empirical trend\" not \"theory/law.\"\n60\t6. **Baseline comparison as evidence for model-level aggregation** — Item-level detection is near-random for ALL binary-only methods (including oracle RF). This is NOT a weakness — it reveals that contamination signal is diffuse (memorization fraction ~35%). Model-level lz* aggregates 14K weak signals into |d|>2.9. Frame item-level weakness as supporting evidence: \"any method that aggregates is better than any method that localizes.\" lz* vs Guttman/U3 8× gap proves IRT parameterization matters. acc z-score is sensitive but non-specific (sensitivity-specificity tradeoff).\n61\t7. **Scope precision** — Target: \"intentional SFT contamination / leaderboard gaming\". Pretraining exposure → smooth distributional improvement, not in scope. Honest scoping > vague claims.\n62\t8. **Terminology guard** — NEVER use \"binary information bottleneck\" or \"information-theoretic lower bound for all binary-only methods.\" We can only claim lz* (a specific statistic) has limitations under SFT generalization — NOT that all binary-only methods fail. Correct framing: \"IRT person-fit as a principled framework at the minimal-information operating point.\"\n63\t9. **Memorization fraction cross-family consistency** — MF is 35–38% at 15% contamination across Qwen (0.38) and Llama (0.35), only 3pp difference. Consistency strengthens generalizability. MF may vary with contamination mechanism (full FT vs QLoRA), model capacity, and benchmark difficulty — acknowledge in Limitations as deployment bootstrapping problem.\n64\t10. **Validation hierarchy** — ctrl-contam = primary validation (ground truth, same model clean vs contaminated); 12-model case study = empirical findings (no ground truth, but validated parameters); v1 data = future work roadmap. Paper structure should reflect this priority.\n65\t9. **lz* measures memorization, not generalization** — lz* detects aberrant patterns (memorization signature), not smooth ability improvement (generalization). This is a feature: it distinguishes genuine learning from rote memorization. Power function gives upper bound; ctrl-contam measures actual memorization fraction.\n66\t10. **Negative results as contributions** — D007 (universal misfit) and D008 (empirical null well-calibrated) are \"honest negative results that become positive contributions\": universal misfit is a novel finding about LLM response patterns; the simulation-reality gap motivates Δlz* and quantifies SFT generalization. D011 (MML-EM refit failure) is a methodological caveat (classical vs neural IRT sample requirements). D009 (item alignment) is a data engineering detail (1 sentence in Data section). Present negatives with the framing: \"the gap between where IRT works perfectly (simulation) and where it struggles (real LLM data) is itself informative.\"\n67\t11. **ctrl-contam actual results (D015)** — contam_15 |d|=3.85 (strong), contam_50 non-monotonic under free-θ but **monotonic under θ-fixed** (ratio=2.93). θ-shift is the mechanism: contamination→higher θ_MLE→absorbs aberrance signal. Frame as \"principled detection with quantified operating characteristics + θ-shift boundary condition.\" Headline: \"We don't just detect — we characterize, including the conditions under which detection degrades.\" Do NOT claim \"state-of-the-art detection.\"\n68\t12. **θ-shift as methodological contribution (D015)** — Person-fit sensitivity degrades when θ is re-estimated on contaminated data. This is a novel finding for contamination detection: (a) θ-fixed analysis recovers monotonic dose-response, (b) the θ-shift magnitude (0.47 at 50% contamination) quantifies the confound, (c) practical implication: use pre-contamination θ baseline or cross-validated θ. Present as a contribution, not a limitation.\n69\t13. **Cross-family baseline lz* divergence (Reviewer WARNING)** — Clean lz* spans ~9σ: Qwen +8.68, Mistral +5.42, Llama -0.14. 固定阈值检测失效（Qwen contam_15 lz*=+3.85 仍为正值）。论文必须：(a) 明确 IRT-FIT 是相对检测（Δlz*/Cohen's d），非绝对阈值；(b) 展示 clean baseline lz* 跨家族差异表；(c) 预防性回答\"contam_15 lz*=+3.85 为正值为何算污染\"（因为 Δlz*=-4.83 是大效应）；(d) lz* baseline 作为\"模型内部一致性\"指标——Mistral acc 最低(0.514)但 lz* 第二高(+5.42)，说明 lz* 捕捉 difficulty-ordering 一致性而非准确率。\n70\t14. **Inverted-U 是模型相关的** — Qwen 在 15%→50% 区间出现强 inversion（Δlz* 50%=-1.39 << 15%=-4.83），Llama 和 Mistral 在此区间 Δlz* 单调增大。Inverted-U 在 free-θ 下由 baseline misfit 调制——高 baseline lz* (Qwen +8.68) 的模型更容易出现 non-monotonicity。论文不能 claim inverted-U 为 universal phenomenon，需加 model-dependent caveat。θ-fixed 分析仍恢复所有家族的 monotonicity — 这是 θ-fixed 贡献的关键证据。\n71\t15. **seen/unseen split as strongest signal** — contam_15 seen lz*≈0 (memorized items look \"normal\") vs unseen lz*=3.1. This is the clearest contamination fingerprint: items that should produce aberrant responses instead produce expected responses. More interpretable than aggregate Δlz*.\n72\t\n73\t## Writing Strategy (from Distillation)\n74\t\n75\t- **Story type**: \"跨界迁移型\" — unused in 0/8 peer papers. Our unique angle.\n76\t- **Figure 1**: Problem illustration (dual-panel: contaminated vs clean response pattern). Use psychometric hook — impossible response pattern (hard items right, easy wrong).\n77\t- **Contribution structure**: 3 contributions (method + characterization + empirical findings), escalating scope. Standard in 5/8 papers.\n78\t- **Result tables**: Row=methods, col=datasets×metrics. Bold-best. Average column.\n79\t- **Numbered findings**: Adopt PSN-IRT's \"Key Discoveries\" strategy for scannable presentation.\n80\t- **CIs as differentiator**: 6/8 peer papers report no variance. Our bootstrap CIs are a competitive advantage.\n81\t- **Baseline count**: Peer median ~5. Target ≥5 baselines.\n82\t\n83\t## Evidence Summary\n84\t\n85\t- **Phase A (simulation)**: Null check passed (4PL compatible with lz* theory). MMLU power@15%=97.9%. Power ∝ n_items confirmed. Simulation results stand — they establish theoretical detection capacity within the 4PL model.\n86\t- **Phase B (real data)**: Gemini-1.5 MMLU acc=1.000, θ=0.78, lz*=+31.81. Cross-benchmark anomaly profiles identified.\n87\t- **Empirical null analysis (exp-empirical-null)**: Bootstrap null calibration is benchmark-size dependent — large benchmarks well-calibrated (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3%; HellaSwag: 8.3%), small benchmarks poorly calibrated (HumanEval: 75%, ARC-C: 58.3%; overall KS reject 40%, 24/60). The statistical framework is sound for large benchmarks (n>10K). 57/60 real-data cells flagged because LLM lz* values are genuinely extreme (|lz*| >> 5) — this reflects **universal 4PL model misfit** (LLM response patterns violate IRT assumptions), not contamination-specific detection. **Important**: 57/60 vs accuracy z-score 4/60 demonstrates lz* captures response-pattern information beyond aggregate accuracy (evidence for Claim 3a universal misfit), NOT contamination detection superiority. Contamination detection evidence comes from simulation (lz* AUROC 0.675 vs ML 0.575 at 5%) and ctrl-contam (d=3.42, n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected).\n88\t- **Calibration gap reframed (D007→D008)**: The gap is not \"null is biased\" but \"real LLM data genuinely deviates from 4PL\". This strengthens Δlz* approach: well-calibrated null means within-model shifts are statistically meaningful.\n89\t- **Controlled contamination (exp-ctrl-contam-pilot, D015, ℓz*-corrected)**: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct × MMLU, 4 conditions (0%×3 seeds, 15%, 50%). **n=3 clean seeds (ℓz*-corrected)**: s1=9.47, s2=7.05, s3=9.53 (s2 outlier; mean=8.68, std=1.41). Key results: (a) contam_15 |Cohen's d|=3.42 (n=3, ddof=1, strong effect); (b) contam_50 |d|=0.99 (n=3, ddof=1, borderline large); (c) free-θ dose-response non-monotonic and more extreme after correction (contam_50 Δlz*=1.40 < contam_15 Δlz*=4.84, ratio=3.46) due to θ-shift confound; (d) **θ-fixed analysis (ℓz*-corrected)**: θ_fixed=-0.0965 (n=3 clean θ_MLE mean, unchanged by ℓz* correction); lz*_fixed: contam_15=11.852, contam_50=18.576; Δlz*_fixed: contam_15=+3.17, contam_50=+9.89; ratio=3.12 (virtually unchanged from uncorrected 3.12 — Snijders correction scales proportionally); (e) seen/unseen memorization fingerprint; (f) memorization_fraction=0.38 (15%) and 0.29 (50%). θ-shift mechanism: contamination raises θ_MLE, absorbing aberrance signal — this is a methodological contribution. **Note**: all values ℓz*-corrected (Snijders 2001); prior uncorrected ℓz values archived as *_lz_uncorrected.json.\n90\t- **Cross-family validation (exp-ctrl-contam-llama, ℓz*-corrected)**: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct × MMLU, 3+2 conditions. **n=3 clean seeds (ℓz*-corrected)**: s1=0.845, s2=-0.182, s3=-1.084 (mean=-0.14, std=0.97). contam_15 lz*=-2.992 (θ=-0.276), contam_50 lz*=-3.331 (θ=-0.149). Δlz*: contam_15=-3.84, contam_50=-4.18, both significant. **Cohen's d (n=3, ddof=1)**: contam_15 d=-2.95 (large), contam_50 d=-3.31 (large), monotonic (|d_50|>|d_15|) — contrasting Qwen's non-monotonicity. **Free-θ**: slight monotonicity (ratio=1.09), contrasting Qwen's strong non-monotonicity (ratio=3.46). **θ_fixed (θ=-0.666)**: lz*_fixed contam_15=0.959, contam_50=1.172; Δlz*_fixed=+0.114/+0.327; ratio=2.87 (enhances monotonicity). θ-shift mechanism universal: Δθ Llama 0.39/0.52, Qwen 0.29/0.47. Memorization fraction ~0.33-0.38. **Narrative**: Qwen story = \"θ_fixed repairs non-monotonicity\"; Llama story = \"θ_fixed enhances existing monotonicity\". Both confirm θ_fixed recovers/strengthens dose-response. Non-monotonicity is model-dependent (modulated by baseline misfit magnitude). Qwen clean lz*=8.68 vs Llama -0.14 — discuss in §6. **Cross-family Cohen's d summary**: Qwen d_15=-3.42/d_50=-0.99; Llama d_15=-2.95/d_50=-3.31. Both families |d|>2.9 at 15%, confirming detection generalizes.\n91\t- **4PL real-data degradation**: 3PL(d=1) vs 4PL lz* correlation r=0.225, RMSE=68.41. d parameter essential for high-ability models (DeepSeek-V3 Δ=357). Complements simulation ablation (+14-27pp power gain).\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ba4edb1-d36b-4522-8fe1-2e7b2b802323","uuid":"27981776-b099-4fdf-8694-3a49d36f739f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:03:26.207Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122444/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — irt_personfit_contam\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (用户确认；ARR submission DDL 5/25 — 需确认可行性)\n- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (abstract 7/21, full paper 7/28, ~10 weeks)\n- Backup: ICLR 2027 (~Sep 2026, ~17 weeks)\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n**Claim 1 (Minimal-Information Detection Framework + Controlled Validation)**:\nIRT person-fit statistics provide a principled contamination detection framework at the minimal-information operating point — requiring only binary response patterns. Simulation establishes high theoretical detection power (97.9% on MMLU-scale benchmarks), and bootstrap null distributions are well-calibrated for large benchmarks (MMLU/HellaSwag KS reject 8.3%, but overall 40% due to small benchmarks like HumanEval 75%, ARC-C 58.3% — calibration degrades with n_items). In simulation, lz* matches or outperforms supervised binary classifiers (LR/RF with oracle labels) at low contamination (5-10%), leveraging IRT structure as inductive bias. Controlled contamination experiments (QLoRA SFT injection on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) validate the framework across model families: Qwen at 15% contamination yields |Cohen's d|=3.42 (n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected) with clear seen/unseen memorization fingerprint; Llama confirms cross-family detection with |Cohen's d|=2.95 at 15% and 3.31 at 50% (n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected), both large effects. θ-fixed analysis reveals a θ-shift boundary condition: when θ is re-estimated on contaminated data, detection sensitivity can degrade (model-dependent; Qwen shows free-θ non-monotonicity due to high baseline misfit, Llama does not), but fixing θ to pre-contamination baseline restores monotonic dose-response in both families. The memorization fraction (~35-38% at 15% contamination, consistent across families) quantifies the SFT generalization effect.\n\n**Claim 2 (Detection Characterization)**:\nDetection power increases with benchmark size (n_items) and varies with contamination level, following an empirical power function (R²=0.65) that provides approximate practical guidance for practitioners assessing detectability on benchmarks of known size — but should NOT be described as \"predictive theory\" or \"scaling law.\" Two distinct signal regimes: partial contamination produces aberrant patterns (lz* << 0), full contamination produces over-consistent patterns (lz* >> 0). For sufficiently large benchmarks (≥10K items), theoretical power exceeds 90% at 15% contamination. Note: power function gives an **upper bound** (assumes all contaminated items produce aberrant signal); SFT generalization reduces effective signal — ctrl-contam measures actual memorization fraction to derive adjusted (\"expected\") power. Both upper bound and adjusted power should be reported. **Baseline comparison (exp-baseline-comparison)**: At model level, lz* achieves 8× the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman/U3) on identical data (|d|=3.42 vs |d|=0.43 for Qwen 15%), demonstrating the value of full 4PL IRT parameterization. At item level, ALL binary-only methods achieve near-random discrimination (AUROC 0.52–0.63, including oracle supervised classifiers), revealing contamination produces a diffuse statistical signature detectable only through model-level aggregation over thousands of items. Accuracy z-score trivially detects in controlled settings (z=160 due to near-zero accuracy variance) but lacks specificity in real-world scenarios. The IRT framework enables principled aggregation by calibrating each item's expected response probability.\n\n**Claim 3 (Empirical Findings from Real LLM Data)**:\nThree empirical findings from applying IRT person-fit to real LLM response data: (a) **Universal 4PL model misfit** — nearly all (model, benchmark) cells produce extreme lz* values (|lz*| >> 5, 57/60 cells flagged after BH-FDR correction), revealing that LLM response patterns fundamentally differ from classical test-taker patterns. This is a genuine property of LLM responses (bootstrap null is well-calibrated), not a statistical artifact. The simulation-to-reality gap motivates the Δlz* design. (b) **PSN-IRT 12-model case study** — detailed anomaly profiles with validated 4PL parameters (Gemini-1.5: 100% MMLU accuracy, lz*=+31.81, θ=0.78; selective vs uniform misfit patterns across benchmarks; Ward clustering reveals contamination typology). (c) **IRT parameter estimation challenges** — MML-EM refit from v1 leaderboard data (N=63→178, covering all available models) yields b_r=0.66 (partial difficulty validation) but a_r≈0.13 (discrimination failure, well below 0.2 threshold), quantifying the sample size gap between classical IRT and neural IRT estimation in LLM contexts. 13K+ model per-item data is publicly available for future large-scale audits once estimation challenges are resolved.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Benchmark contamination undermines LLM evaluation reliability. As benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow, contamination risk increases, but all existing detection methods require privileged access — training data (n-gram overlap), token probabilities (Min-K%, MIA), model weights (internal probing), or specially crafted prompts (Time Travel). A contaminated LLM is functionally analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge, yet no work has connected the mature psychometric literature on cheating detection to LLM benchmark integrity.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods circa 2026 span five families: time-based partition, MIA/Min-K%++ (ICLR 2024), Time Travel probing (ICLR 2024 Spotlight), ConStat performance-based testing (NeurIPS 2024), and CoDeC in-context learning effects (ICLR 2026). Wang et al. (2025) show all MIA-based methods fail under post-RL contamination. Critically, ALL require more than binary response patterns — no method operates at the minimal-information level.\n\n**Our framing**: We establish the minimal-information operating point on the contamination detection frontier. By repurposing IRT person-fit statistics — developed over decades for detecting exam cheating — we ask: \"What can be detected from binary response patterns alone?\" We show: (1) in simulation, person-fit statistics achieve high detection power (97.9% on MMLU-scale benchmarks) with well-calibrated null distributions; (2) in real LLM data, response patterns universally deviate from 4PL predictions — the statistical framework is sound, but LLMs genuinely violate IRT assumptions (local independence, unidimensionality), producing extreme lz* values across nearly all models; (3) controlled contamination experiments leverage this by measuring Δlz* between clean and contaminated models — since the null is well-calibrated, within-model shifts are statistically interpretable. The universal model misfit finding is itself a contribution — it reveals that LLM response patterns are fundamentally different from classical test-taker patterns.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"Using only binary response patterns from the target model (with externally calibrated IRT item parameters), IRT-FIT detects controlled contamination with |Cohen's d|>2.9 across two model families (Qwen, Llama), achieving 8× the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman/U3). The HT statistic (requiring only item difficulty) achieves comparable model-level detection power — the IRT person-fit family collectively dominates simpler indices, with ℓz*'s advantage lying in mechanistic interpretability (θ-shift analysis, memorization fraction quantification) and [pending: post-RL robustness vs HT]. Model-level aggregation over thousands of items yields robust detection where all item-level methods are near-random (AUROC 0.52–0.63). The IRT framework provides unique mechanistic insight: θ-shift analysis reveals how contamination raises ability estimates, and memorization fraction (35–38%) bridges theoretical power (97.9%) and observed detection capacity.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP**: NLP evaluation methodology fits well. Risk: \"no new NLP model/technique\" → mitigate by positioning as foundational evaluation methodology with direct community impact (leaderboard integrity).\n- **AAAI**: PSN-IRT was AAAI 2026 Oral — strong psychometrics+AI audience. Risk: less NLP-specific reviewer pool.\n- **Cross-discipline concern**: IRT/psychometrics may be unfamiliar to ML reviewers → need self-contained exposition in Section 3 (IRT background), avoid jargon, provide intuitive explanations.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n\n| # | Concern | Response Strategy | Supporting Experiment |\n|---|---------|------------------|----------------------|\n| R1 | \"Just applying existing IRT to LLMs\" | Frame as establishing new operating point, not cross-domain transfer. Person-fit for contamination is unexplored. Downgrade \"cross-domain\" from contribution to background. | — |\n| R2 | \"No ground truth contamination labels\" | Controlled contamination experiment (fine-tune clean models on benchmark items). Phase B anomalies are converging evidence, not proof. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n| R3 | \"Power too low for small benchmarks\" | Present power function analysis as contribution, not limitation. Derive theoretical relationship. | exp-power-theory |\n| R4 | \"Only 12 models\" | 12 models with validated 4PL parameters provide detailed anomaly profiles. Universal misfit (57/60 flagged) spans sufficient models to establish the pattern. 13K+ v1 per-item data availability discussed as future work. Ctrl-contam covers 4-5 model families. | exp-ctrl-contam, phase_b_personfit |\n| R5 | \"SFT generalization smooths signal\" | Controlled experiment includes domain-level analysis. Discuss hard-item contamination resistance. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n| R6 | \"Absolute lz* thresholds don't work on real data\" | Bootstrap null is well-calibrated (KS pass for large benchmarks); the issue is LLMs genuinely deviate from 4PL. Universal model misfit is a finding, not an artifact. Δlz* leverages the well-calibrated null for within-model comparisons. | exp-ctrl-contam, exp-empirical-null |\n| R7 | \"Post-RL contamination?\" | Hypothesis test: GRPO after SFT contamination on Qwen 3.5-9B. | exp-post-rl |\n| R8 | \"4PL vs 3PL — does d parameter matter?\" | Ablation comparing 4PL vs 3PL(d=1) detection sensitivity. | exp-4pl-ablation |\n| R9 | \"No same-level binary-only baselines\" | **Completed (exp-baseline-comparison)**: Model-level: lz* |d|=3.42 vs Guttman/U3 |d|=0.43 (8× gap) — full IRT parameterization far exceeds difficulty-only person-fit. **HT is nearly as powerful as ℓz* (avg |d| comparable)** — the \"8×\" headline must include HT comparison to avoid selective framing. Item-level: all methods near-random (AUROC 0.52–0.63). acc z-score detects trivially (z=160) but lacks specificity. | exp-baseline-comparison |\n| R12 | \"HT ≈ ℓz* — why use ℓz*?\" | **[Dream D2 三档一致]** HT achieves comparable |d| with weaker requirements (difficulty only, no 4PL). **四场景应对 (D022, Reviewer validated)**：(Best) HT post-RL 丢失 + ℓz* 保留 → headline 差异化；(Good) HT 弱化 + ℓz* 更强 → quantitative comparison；(Neutral) 均保留 → 转用数据无关差异化（见下）；(Bad) 均丢失 → negative-as-contribution, scope SFT。**数据无关差异化（任何场景均可用）**：(i) ℓz* 有参数化 null (≈N(0,1), bootstrap 已验证) → 可直接假设检验, HT 无此性质；(ii) Snijders 校正补偿 θ 估计误差 (Var*=Var_l - C²/I(θ)), HT 无此校正；(iii) ℓz* 用 a/b/c/d 四参数加权 item 贡献, HT 仅用 b → MMLU 跨 57 科目 item 异质性大时 ℓz* 理论更精确。这些点不依赖 post-RL 结果，论文中应明确讨论。 | exp-baseline-comparison, exp-post-rl |\n| R13 | \"Minimal-information overstates\" | **[Dream D2 三档一致]** \"Minimal-information operating point\" obscures PSN-IRT 4PL parameter dependency. Reframe to \"minimal information **from the target model**\" or \"minimal runtime information\" — explicitly acknowledge PSN-IRT calibration as an external input requirement. | — |\n| R10 | \"Leaderboard audit baseline unreliable\" | Leaderboard audit abandoned (D011). ctrl-contam is the validated detection. 12-model case study uses PSN-IRT validated parameters directly (no baseline estimation needed). v1 data availability noted as future work. | exp-ctrl-contam |\n| R11 | \"Power function is upper bound\" | Report both theoretical upper bound AND adjusted power (memorization fraction from ctrl-contam seen/unseen split). Gap = SFT generalization effect quantification. | exp-ctrl-contam, exp-power-theory |\n\n## Key Narrative Elements (from External Review + Distillation)\n\n1. **Detection frontier framing** — Systematically rank all methods by information access level (binary < rephrased retest < token probs < logits < weights). IRT-FIT defines the bottom of this frontier.\n2. **Definitional pivot (à la ConStat)** — \"All prior contamination detection methods require privileged access. We ask a more fundamental question: what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?\" This reframes our work as problem reformulation, not just cross-domain transfer.\n3. **RL-robustness as structural advantage** — Wang et al. (ICLR 2026) proves all 10 log-prob methods ≈ random on RL-trained reasoning models. Person-fit operates on binary response patterns, not token distributions — structurally immune to RL-induced distributional shifts. This is our strongest motivation against log-prob baselines.\n4. **Scalability potential (deferred to future work)** — 13K+ v1 per-item data publicly available. IRT-FIT is uniquely suited for retrospective audit (only needs archival binary data). BUT: current paper does NOT include large-scale audit (D011: IRT refit failed). Mention data availability + roadmap in Discussion/Future Work. NEVER claim \"leaderboard audit\" or \"large-scale detection\" in current paper.\n5. **Inverted-U as empirical characterization** — Derive from: (a) contamination→0%: signal drowned in sampling noise; (b) contamination→100%: identifiability collapse. Simulation fit R²=0.957; real-data fit R²=0.65 (gap due to SFT generalization). Frame as \"empirical trend\" not \"theory/law.\"\n6. **Baseline comparison as evidence for model-level aggregation** — Item-level detection is near-random for ALL binary-only methods (including oracle RF). This is NOT a weakness — it reveals that contamination signal is diffuse (memorization fraction ~35%). Model-level lz* aggregates 14K weak signals into |d|>2.9. Frame item-level weakness as supporting evidence: \"any method that aggregates is better than any method that localizes.\" lz* vs Guttman/U3 8× gap proves IRT parameterization matters. acc z-score is sensitive but non-specific (sensitivity-specificity tradeoff).\n7. **Scope precision** — Target: \"intentional SFT contamination / leaderboard gaming\". Pretraining exposure → smooth distributional improvement, not in scope. Honest scoping > vague claims.\n8. **Terminology guard** — NEVER use \"binary information bottleneck\" or \"information-theoretic lower bound for all binary-only methods.\" We can only claim lz* (a specific statistic) has limitations under SFT generalization — NOT that all binary-only methods fail. Correct framing: \"IRT person-fit as a principled framework at the minimal-information operating point.\"\n9. **Memorization fraction cross-family consistency** — MF is 35–38% at 15% contamination across Qwen (0.38) and Llama (0.35), only 3pp difference. Consistency strengthens generalizability. MF may vary with contamination mechanism (full FT vs QLoRA), model capacity, and benchmark difficulty — acknowledge in Limitations as deployment bootstrapping problem.\n10. **Validation hierarchy** — ctrl-contam = primary validation (ground truth, same model clean vs contaminated); 12-model case study = empirical findings (no ground truth, but validated parameters); v1 data = future work roadmap. Paper structure should reflect this priority.\n9. **lz* measures memorization, not generalization** — lz* detects aberrant patterns (memorization signature), not smooth ability improvement (generalization). This is a feature: it distinguishes genuine learning from rote memorization. Power function gives upper bound; ctrl-contam measures actual memorization fraction.\n10. **Negative results as contributions** — D007 (universal misfit) and D008 (empirical null well-calibrated) are \"honest negative results that become positive contributions\": universal misfit is a novel finding about LLM response patterns; the simulation-reality gap motivates Δlz* and quantifies SFT generalization. D011 (MML-EM refit failure) is a methodological caveat (classical vs neural IRT sample requirements). D009 (item alignment) is a data engineering detail (1 sentence in Data section). Present negatives with the framing: \"the gap between where IRT works perfectly (simulation) and where it struggles (real LLM data) is itself informative.\"\n11. **ctrl-contam actual results (D015)** — contam_15 |d|=3.85 (strong), contam_50 non-monotonic under free-θ but **monotonic under θ-fixed** (ratio=2.93). θ-shift is the mechanism: contamination→higher θ_MLE→absorbs aberrance signal. Frame as \"principled detection with quantified operating characteristics + θ-shift boundary condition.\" Headline: \"We don't just detect — we characterize, including the conditions under which detection degrades.\" Do NOT claim \"state-of-the-art detection.\"\n12. **θ-shift as methodological contribution (D015)** — Person-fit sensitivity degrades when θ is re-estimated on contaminated data. This is a novel finding for contamination detection: (a) θ-fixed analysis recovers monotonic dose-response, (b) the θ-shift magnitude (0.47 at 50% contamination) quantifies the confound, (c) practical implication: use pre-contamination θ baseline or cross-validated θ. Present as a contribution, not a limitation.\n13. **Cross-family baseline lz* divergence (Reviewer WARNING)** — Clean lz* spans ~9σ: Qwen +8.68, Mistral +5.42, Llama -0.14. 固定阈值检测失效（Qwen contam_15 lz*=+3.85 仍为正值）。论文必须：(a) 明确 IRT-FIT 是相对检测（Δlz*/Cohen's d），非绝对阈值；(b) 展示 clean baseline lz* 跨家族差异表；(c) 预防性回答\"contam_15 lz*=+3.85 为正值为何算污染\"（因为 Δlz*=-4.83 是大效应）；(d) lz* baseline 作为\"模型内部一致性\"指标——Mistral acc 最低(0.514)但 lz* 第二高(+5.42)，说明 lz* 捕捉 difficulty-ordering 一致性而非准确率。\n14. **Inverted-U 是模型相关的** — Qwen 在 15%→50% 区间出现强 inversion（Δlz* 50%=-1.39 << 15%=-4.83），Llama 和 Mistral 在此区间 Δlz* 单调增大。Inverted-U 在 free-θ 下由 baseline misfit 调制——高 baseline lz* (Qwen +8.68) 的模型更容易出现 non-monotonicity。论文不能 claim inverted-U 为 universal phenomenon，需加 model-dependent caveat。θ-fixed 分析仍恢复所有家族的 monotonicity — 这是 θ-fixed 贡献的关键证据。\n15. **seen/unseen split as strongest signal** — contam_15 seen lz*≈0 (memorized items look \"normal\") vs unseen lz*=3.1. This is the clearest contamination fingerprint: items that should produce aberrant responses instead produce expected responses. More interpretable than aggregate Δlz*.\n\n## Writing Strategy (from Distillation)\n\n- **Story type**: \"跨界迁移型\" — unused in 0/8 peer papers. Our unique angle.\n- **Figure 1**: Problem illustration (dual-panel: contaminated vs clean response pattern). Use psychometric hook — impossible response pattern (hard items right, easy wrong).\n- **Contribution structure**: 3 contributions (method + characterization + empirical findings), escalating scope. Standard in 5/8 papers.\n- **Result tables**: Row=methods, col=datasets×metrics. Bold-best. Average column.\n- **Numbered findings**: Adopt PSN-IRT's \"Key Discoveries\" strategy for scannable presentation.\n- **CIs as differentiator**: 6/8 peer papers report no variance. Our bootstrap CIs are a competitive advantage.\n- **Baseline count**: Peer median ~5. Target ≥5 baselines.\n\n## Evidence Summary\n\n- **Phase A (simulation)**: Null check passed (4PL compatible with lz* theory). MMLU power@15%=97.9%. Power ∝ n_items confirmed. Simulation results stand — they establish theoretical detection capacity within the 4PL model.\n- **Phase B (real data)**: Gemini-1.5 MMLU acc=1.000, θ=0.78, lz*=+31.81. Cross-benchmark anomaly profiles identified.\n- **Empirical null analysis (exp-empirical-null)**: Bootstrap null calibration is benchmark-size dependent — large benchmarks well-calibrated (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3%; HellaSwag: 8.3%), small benchmarks poorly calibrated (HumanEval: 75%, ARC-C: 58.3%; overall KS reject 40%, 24/60). The statistical framework is sound for large benchmarks (n>10K). 57/60 real-data cells flagged because LLM lz* values are genuinely extreme (|lz*| >> 5) — this reflects **universal 4PL model misfit** (LLM response patterns violate IRT assumptions), not contamination-specific detection. **Important**: 57/60 vs accuracy z-score 4/60 demonstrates lz* captures response-pattern information beyond aggregate accuracy (evidence for Claim 3a universal misfit), NOT contamination detection superiority. Contamination detection evidence comes from simulation (lz* AUROC 0.675 vs ML 0.575 at 5%) and ctrl-contam (d=3.42, n=3, ddof=1, ℓz*-corrected).\n- **Calibration gap reframed (D007→D008)**: The gap is not \"null is biased\" but \"real LLM data genuinely deviates from 4PL\". This strengthens Δlz* approach: well-calibrated null means within-model shifts are statistically meaningful.\n- **Controlled contamination (exp-ctrl-contam-pilot, D015, ℓz*-corrected)**: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct × MMLU, 4 conditions (0%×3 seeds, 15%, 50%). **n=3 clean seeds (ℓz*-corrected)**: s1=9.47, s2=7.05, s3=9.53 (s2 outlier; mean=8.68, std=1.41). Key results: (a) contam_15 |Cohen's d|=3.42 (n=3, ddof=1, strong effect); (b) contam_50 |d|=0.99 (n=3, ddof=1, borderline large); (c) free-θ dose-response non-monotonic and more extreme after correction (contam_50 Δlz*=1.40 < contam_15 Δlz*=4.84, ratio=3.46) due to θ-shift confound; (d) **θ-fixed analysis (ℓz*-corrected)**: θ_fixed=-0.0965 (n=3 clean θ_MLE mean, unchanged by ℓz* correction); lz*_fixed: contam_15=11.852, contam_50=18.576; Δlz*_fixed: contam_15=+3.17, contam_50=+9.89; ratio=3.12 (virtually unchanged from uncorrected 3.12 — Snijders correction scales proportionally); (e) seen/unseen memorization fingerprint; (f) memorization_fraction=0.38 (15%) and 0.29 (50%). θ-shift mechanism: contamination raises θ_MLE, absorbing aberrance signal — this is a methodological contribution. **Note**: all values ℓz*-corrected (Snijders 2001); prior uncorrected ℓz values archived as *_lz_uncorrected.json.\n- **Cross-family validation (exp-ctrl-contam-llama, ℓz*-corrected)**: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct × MMLU, 3+2 conditions. **n=3 clean seeds (ℓz*-corrected)**: s1=0.845, s2=-0.182, s3=-1.084 (mean=-0.14, std=0.97). contam_15 lz*=-2.992 (θ=-0.276), contam_50 lz*=-3.331 (θ=-0.149). Δlz*: contam_15=-3.84, contam_50=-4.18, both significant. **Cohen's d (n=3, ddof=1)**: contam_15 d=-2.95 (large), contam_50 d=-3.31 (large), monotonic (|d_50|>|d_15|) — contrasting Qwen's non-monotonicity. **Free-θ**: slight monotonicity (ratio=1.09), contrasting Qwen's strong non-monotonicity (ratio=3.46). **θ_fixed (θ=-0.666)**: lz*_fixed contam_15=0.959, contam_50=1.172; Δlz*_fixed=+0.114/+0.327; ratio=2.87 (enhances monotonicity). θ-shift mechanism universal: Δθ Llama 0.39/0.52, Qwen 0.29/0.47. Memorization fraction ~0.33-0.38. **Narrative**: Qwen story = \"θ_fixed repairs non-monotonicity\"; Llama story = \"θ_fixed enhances existing monotonicity\". Both confirm θ_fixed recovers/strengthens dose-response. Non-monotonicity is model-dependent (modulated by baseline misfit magnitude). Qwen clean lz*=8.68 vs Llama -0.14 — discuss in §6. **Cross-family Cohen's d summary**: Qwen d_15=-3.42/d_50=-0.99; Llama d_15=-2.95/d_50=-3.31. Both families |d|>2.9 at 15%, confirming detection generalizes.\n- **4PL real-data degradation**: 3PL(d=1) vs 4PL lz* correlation r=0.225, RMSE=68.41. d parameter essential for high-ability models (DeepSeek-V3 Δ=357). Complements simulation ablation (+14-27pp power gain).\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011LnGFpXPnJfD3Uv9FHMoXv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Controlled Contamination Pilot (Qwen × MMLU)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct × 4 conditions (0%-seed1/0%-seed2/15%/50%) × MMLU. Go/no-go signal for the paper. Measures Δlz*, clean noise floor, memorization fraction.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"n_clean_seeds\": 2,\n7\t      \"clean_seed1_lz_star\": 8.9,\n8\t      \"clean_seed2_lz_star\": 8.4,\n9\t      \"clean_vs_clean_delta_lz\": 0.5,\n10\t      \"clean_mean_lz_star\": 8.65,\n11\t      \"clean_std_lz_star\": 0.35,\n12\t      \"contam_15_lz_star\": 4.1,\n13\t      \"contam_50_lz_star\": 7.5,\n14\t      \"delta_lz_contam15\": -4.55,\n15\t      \"delta_lz_contam50\": -1.15,\n16\t      \"cohens_d_contam15\": -3.1,\n17\t      \"cohens_d_contam50\": -0.9,\n18\t      \"theta_shift_15\": 0.28,\n19\t      \"theta_shift_50\": 0.45,\n20\t      \"memorization_fraction_15\": 0.36,\n21\t      \"memorization_fraction_50\": 0.3,\n22\t      \"theta_fixed_contam15_lz\": 11.5,\n23\t      \"theta_fixed_contam50_lz\": 17.8,\n24\t      \"theta_fixed_ratio\": 3.0,\n25\t      \"ht_d_contam15\": -3.0,\n26\t      \"ht_d_contam50\": -0.85,\n27\t      \"acc_contam15\": 0.71,\n28\t      \"acc_contam50\": 0.725,\n29\t      \"acc_seen_contam15\": 0.78,\n30\t      \"acc_unseen_contam15\": 0.68\n31\t    },\n32\t    \"tables\": [\n33\t      \"Table: Pilot results — lz* and HT Cohen's d across conditions, with θ-free and θ-fixed\"\n34\t    ],\n35\t    \"figures\": [],\n36\t    \"analysis\": \"Go signal confirmed for SFT scope. But HT matching lz* is a warning: the simpler statistic captures the same information at model level. Non-monotonicity under free-θ motivates θ-fixed methodology. Paper can claim SFT detection with |d|>2.5 but must acknowledge HT parity.\\n\\n\\nSFT with QLoRA directly injects benchmark items into training. Model memorizes ~36% of seen items (consistent with QLoRA's partial parameter update — full FT would memorize more). At 15%, 2106 items are seen; ~758 are memorized, creating a diffuse aberrance signal detectable at model level. Cohen's d ~3.1 because lz* aggregates 14042 item-level weak signals. Non-monotonicity at 50% (|d|=0.9 < 3.1) because θ-shift absorbs aberrance: contaminating 50% raises θ_MLE by ~0.45, making previously-aberrant correct answers look expected. θ-fixed analysis restores monotonicity (ratio ~3.0). HT matches lz* (d ~3.0 vs 3.1) because the dominant signal is difficulty-ordering violation — items the model \\\"should\\\" get wrong are answered correctly — and HT captures this with only item difficulty. The full 4PL parameterization (a, c, d) adds negligible incremental signal at model level when the difficulty violation is already large. Clean-vs-clean Δlz* ~0.5 confirms noise floor is small relative to contam signal.\\n\"\n37\t  },\n38\t  {\n39\t    \"name\": \"Full Controlled Contamination (4 Models × 6 Levels × 3 Benchmarks)\",\n40\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Gemma-4-31B, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3 × 0%/5%/10%/15%/25%/50% × MMLU/GSM8K/HellaSwag.\\n\",\n41\t    \"results\": {\n42\t      \"n_families_validated\": 2,\n43\t      \"qwen_mmlu_d15\": -3.1,\n44\t      \"qwen_mmlu_d50\": -0.9,\n45\t      \"llama_mmlu_d15\": -2.7,\n46\t      \"llama_mmlu_d50\": -3.0,\n47\t      \"mistral_mmlu_d15\": -2.4,\n48\t      \"mistral_mmlu_d50\": -2.8,\n49\t      \"gemma_mmlu_d15\": -1.8,\n50\t      \"gemma_mmlu_d50\": -2.2,\n51\t      \"qwen_gsm8k_d15\": -1.4,\n52\t      \"llama_gsm8k_d15\": -1.2,\n53\t      \"qwen_hellaswag_d15\": -1.8,\n54\t      \"llama_hellaswag_d15\": -1.5,\n55\t      \"qwen_ht_mmlu_d15\": -3.0,\n56\t      \"llama_ht_mmlu_d15\": -2.6,\n57\t      \"cross_family_mf_range\": \"0.30-0.40\",\n58\t      \"cross_family_mf_std\": 0.04,\n59\t      \"theta_fixed_monotonic_all\": true,\n60\t      \"dose_response_5pct_detectable\": false,\n61\t      \"dose_response_10pct_detectable\": \"marginal (d ~0.8-1.2 on MMLU only)\",\n62\t      \"min_contam_for_detection_mmlu\": \"10-15%\",\n63\t      \"min_contam_for_detection_gsm8k\": \"25%\",\n64\t      \"min_contam_for_detection_hellaswag\": \"15-25%\"\n65\t    },\n66\t    \"tables\": [\n67\t      \"Table: Cohen's d matrix — rows=models, cols=benchmarks×levels, bold best per column\",\n68\t      \"Table: HT vs lz* Cohen's d comparison — no significant difference in any cell\",\n69\t      \"Table: Memorization fraction across models and levels — 30-40% range\"\n70\t    ],\n71\t    \"figures\": [],\n72\t    \"analysis\": \"SFT detection validated across 2 families with multiple seeds, but cross-family scope is limited. Gemma's weaker signal warns that larger models may approach the detection boundary even for SFT. GSM8K near the detectability threshold at 15%. HT parity across all 4×6×3 conditions eliminates empirical lz* advantage — the paper must rely on data-independent differentiation (parametric null, Snijders correction, 4PL item weighting).\\n\\n\\nOnly 2 families (Qwen + Llama) are fully validated with 3+ clean seeds each — Mistral and Gemma have single clean seeds, insufficient for rigorous Cohen's d with CI. Gemma-4-31B shows weaker detection (d ~1.8) because larger models have more latent capacity to absorb contamination without creating aberrant patterns — the 31B model generalizes from seen items more smoothly than 7-9B models, reducing the memorization fraction to ~0.25. GSM8K (1319 items) and HellaSwag (10042 items) show weaker detection than MMLU (14042 items), consistent with power function — fewer items = less aggregation power. GSM8K is particularly weak because its chain-of-thought format means SFT contamination partially teaches reasoning, not just memorization. HT matches lz* across all conditions (|d_HT - d_lz*| < 0.3) because difficulty-ordering violation dominates in all model-benchmark combinations. At 5% contamination, signal is indistinguishable from noise (only ~700 items seen on MMLU, ~250 memorized — insufficient for model-level detection). Cross-family MF consistency (0.30-0.40, std ~0.04) supports the generalizability of the memorization fraction concept. θ-fixed universally restores monotonicity, confirming this as a model-independent phenomenon.\\n\"\n73\t  },\n74\t  {\n75\t    \"name\": \"Leaderboard Audit (ABANDONED)\",\n76\t    \"description\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard v1 大规模扫描。已放弃因 IRT refit 失败。\\n\",\n77\t    \"results\": {\n78\t      \"status\": \"ABANDONED\",\n79\t      \"reason\": \"a_r=0.111 (discrimination) + item alignment 54.56% → 13K audit infeasible\",\n80\t      \"v1_data_availability\": \"13K+ models with per-item binary data\",\n81\t      \"future_work_note\": \"Data availability mentioned in Discussion; audit deferred to future work pending neural IRT estimation advances\"\n82\t    },\n83\t    \"tables\": [],\n84\t    \"figures\": [],\n85\t    \"analysis\": \"ABANDONED. Not a paper weakness — frame as honest methodological caveat in Section 6 (IRT parameter estimation challenges). The 13K+ data availability is a future-work contribution. Paper uses PSN-IRT validated parameters (external calibration) instead.\\n\\n\\nClassical IRT estimation (MML-EM) with N=63-178 models produces a_r=0.111 (well below 0.2 threshold), making the refitted parameters effectively discrimination-free. lz* computed with these parameters collapses to an accuracy-like metric, losing the IRT advantage. The root cause is that classical IRT assumes large samples of independent examinees, but LLM response patterns violate this (models are not independent draws from a population; they share training data and architectures). Item alignment at 54.56% further degrades parameter quality. This failure is a genuine methodological finding about the gap between classical and neural IRT estimation requirements.\\n\"\n86\t  },\n87\t  {\n88\t    \"name\": \"Natural Experiment (Base→Chat Pairs)\",\n89\t    \"description\": \"v1 leaderboard base→chat model pairs: Δlz* = lz*(chat) - lz*(base) as SFT effect proxy.\\n\",\n90\t    \"results\": {\n91\t      \"n_viable_pairs\": 5,\n92\t      \"hellaswag_mean_deviation\": -3.2,\n93\t      \"hellaswag_p_value\": 0.003,\n94\t      \"gsm8k_mean_deviation\": 3.5,\n95\t      \"gsm8k_p_value\": 0.015,\n96\t      \"mmlu_p_value\": 0.18,\n97\t      \"arcc_p_value\": 0.42,\n98\t      \"individual_outliers_detected\": 0,\n99\t      \"statistical_power_for_individual_detection\": \"insufficient\"\n100\t    },\n101\t    \"tables\": [\n102\t      \"Table: Paired t-test results by benchmark — HellaSwag and GSM8K significant, MMLU/ARC-C not\"\n103\t    ],\n104\t    \"figures\": [],\n105\t    \"analysis\": \"Deprioritized. Systematic SFT effects are a minor observation for Discussion section. No contamination detection capability. Frame as: \\\"natural experiments show SFT consistently changes lz* patterns, motivating the controlled contamination approach.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWith a_r=0.111, lz* is essentially accuracy-weighted, not IRT-weighted. The HellaSwag and GSM8K systematic effects reflect aggregate SFT training dynamics (SFT systematically changes performance patterns on these benchmarks), not contamination-specific signals. Only 5 viable pairs provide no statistical power for detecting individual contamination outliers — even a genuinely contaminated model would not stand out from the systematic SFT effect. MMLU and ARC-C show no significant systematic effect (p>0.1), possibly because SFT effects on these benchmarks are more heterogeneous across model families. The P2-optional priority is justified: results are mildly interesting (systematic SFT effects exist) but contribute nothing to the contamination detection narrative.\\n\"\n106\t  },\n107\t  {\n108\t    \"name\": \"v1 IRT Refit Expanded (Path D Closure)\",\n109\t    \"description\": \"扩大 v1 IRT 重拟合至 N≥500, 验证 a_r=0.111 是否因样本量不足。\\n\",\n110\t    \"results\": {\n111\t      \"n_models_downloaded\": 500,\n112\t      \"3pl_a_pearson_r\": 0.13,\n113\t      \"2pl_a_pearson_r\": -0.02,\n114\t      \"3pl_b_pearson_r\": 0.66,\n115\t      \"a_r_threshold\": 0.2,\n116\t      \"a_r_verdict\": \"dead (a_r < 0.2)\",\n117\t      \"path_d_status\": \"closed\"\n118\t    },\n119\t    \"tables\": [\n120\t      \"Table: IRT refit metrics at N=63, N=178, N=500 — showing a_r plateau below 0.2\"\n121\t    ],\n122\t    \"figures\": [],\n123\t    \"analysis\": \"Path D confirmed dead. Paper Section 6 (Empirical Findings) reports this as a methodological caveat: classical IRT estimation from heterogeneous LLM samples fails on discrimination. Frames the gap between classical (small N, homogeneous) and neural IRT (large N, heterogeneous) estimation as an open problem for the community.\\n\\n\\nExpanding from N=63→178 only improved a_r from 0.111 to 0.127. Extrapolating to N=500, the marginal gain is diminishing (~0.13-0.14). The fundamental issue is not sample size but model heterogeneity: LLMs from different families/scales violate the classical IRT assumption that all examinees are drawn from a single latent trait distribution. A 7B model and a 70B model are not \\\"two examinees differing in ability\\\" — they differ structurally in how they process items. Classical MML-EM cannot recover meaningful discrimination parameters from this heterogeneous sample. b_r stabilizes at ~0.66 because difficulty is a more robust property (hard items are hard for all models), while discrimination is model-architecture-dependent. This definitively closes Path D.\\n\"\n124\t  },\n125\t  {\n126\t    \"name\": \"4PL vs 3PL Ablation\",\n127\t    \"description\": \"4PL vs 3PL(d=1) 检测灵敏度比较。量化 d 参数对 FAR 和 power 的影响。\\n\",\n128\t    \"results\": {\n129\t      \"far_4pl\": 0.05,\n130\t      \"far_3pl\": \"0.05-0.16\",\n131\t      \"far_2pl\": \"0.50-1.00\",\n132\t      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_mmlu\": 0.25,\n133\t      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_hellaswag\": 0.24,\n134\t      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_gsm8k\": 0.19,\n135\t      \"pct_mmlu_items_d_lt_0.95\": 0.46,\n136\t      \"max_power_gain_at_low_d\": 0.55,\n137\t      \"real_data_correlation_4pl_3pl\": 0.23,\n138\t      \"deepseek_delta_lz\": 340\n139\t    },\n140\t    \"tables\": [\n141\t      \"Table: FAR and power by parameterization (2PL/3PL/4PL) × benchmark\",\n142\t      \"Table: Power gain stratified by d-parameter bucket\"\n143\t    ],\n144\t    \"figures\": [],\n145\t    \"analysis\": \"Strong methodological contribution independent of worst-tier narrative. 4PL is essential for LLM contamination detection — this justifies the PSN-IRT parameter dependency. However, since HT (difficulty-only) matches lz* empirically, the 4PL advantage is theoretical (better calibrated null, correct ceiling modeling) rather than empirical detection superiority. Tension: 4PL matters for calibration, but HT doesn't need 4PL and still matches.\\n\\n\\n4PL d parameter models the probability ceiling — P(correct) for very high ability examinees. 46% of MMLU items have d<0.95, meaning even the best models have <95% ceiling probability. When d=1 is forced (3PL), these items' response probabilities approach 1 for high-ability models, making any incorrect answer look extremely aberrant and inflating lz*. This creates both inflated FAR (false aberrance from normal ceiling effects) and reduced detection power (genuine contamination signal is masked by the d-induced noise). The +25pp mean power gain on MMLU reflects 4PL's ability to correctly model ceiling effects so that only genuine difficulty-ordering violations are flagged. 2PL is catastrophic because removing both c (guessing) and d (ceiling) makes the model unable to account for 40-60% of MMLU response variability. This result is tier-independent — it reflects a structural property of IRT parameterization, not a property of the detection frontier.\\n\"\n146\t  },\n147\t  {\n148\t    \"name\": \"Baseline Comparison (≥5 Baselines)\",\n149\t    \"description\": \"IRT-FIT vs 5+ baselines on ground-truth contaminated models: ConStat (re-test), Min-K%++ (token probs), accuracy z-score, PPL-based, HT, Guttman/U3, random.\\n\",\n150\t    \"results\": {\n151\t      \"n_baselines\": 7,\n152\t      \"n_baselines_beaten\": 3,\n153\t      \"model_level_lz_star_d_15\": -3.1,\n154\t      \"model_level_ht_d_15\": -3.0,\n155\t      \"model_level_guttman_d_15\": -0.4,\n156\t      \"model_level_u3_d_15\": -0.45,\n157\t      \"model_level_acc_zscore\": 155,\n158\t      \"constat_retest_auroc\": 0.88,\n159\t      \"mink_sft_auroc\": 0.68,\n160\t      \"ppl_sft_auroc\": 0.63,\n161\t      \"random_auroc\": 0.5,\n162\t      \"item_level_all_auroc_range\": \"0.50-0.63\",\n163\t      \"item_level_best_oracle_rf\": 0.63,\n164\t      \"lz_star_vs_ht_diff\": \"not significant (p>0.3)\",\n165\t      \"bootstrap_ci_width_lz_d\": 1.2\n166\t    },\n167\t    \"tables\": [\n168\t      \"Table: Model-level detection comparison — rows=methods (8), cols=models×conditions, Cohen's d or AUROC\",\n169\t      \"Table: Item-level AUROC — all methods 0.50-0.63, confirming model-level aggregation is key\",\n170\t      \"Table: Information access hierarchy — method, info required, SFT detection, post-RL detection\"\n171\t    ],\n172\t    \"figures\": [],\n173\t    \"analysis\": \"Paper cannot claim detection superiority. Must reframe as: \\\"We establish the minimal-information operating point. Methods with more information access outperform — this is expected and honest. Our contribution is the framework (connecting psychometrics to LLM evaluation) and the characterization (θ-shift, memorization fraction, RL boundary). lz* beats only classical person-fit (Guttman/U3) and random — the 8× gap over Guttman shows IRT parameterization matters, but HT achieves the same with only difficulty.\\\" Bootstrap CIs (6/8 peer papers report none) remain a differentiator.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, lz* only beats 3 baselines: Guttman (d~0.4), U3 (d~0.45), and random (d=0). HT matches lz* (d~3.0 vs 3.1, difference not significant with n=3 clean seeds). ConStat with rephrased re-testing achieves AUROC 0.88 because re-testing provides a fundamentally richer signal — it creates a second independent measurement of the same items, enabling paired comparison. This is strictly more informative than binary-only. Min-K++ achieves SFT AUROC 0.68 because token-level probability distributions contain granular memorization signatures (specific logit patterns on memorized items) that binary responses discard. PPL-based detection gets 0.63 on SFT (above random but weak). All item-level methods are near-random (0.50-0.63) including oracle RF, confirming that contamination signal is diffuse — memorization fraction ~35% means 65% of seen items show no memorization signal. acc z-score achieves z~155 (trivially detects) but is non-specific: it can't distinguish contamination from genuine ability. The key worst-tier finding: methods with more information access (ConStat > Min-K++ > PPL > lz*) consistently outperform. lz*'s value is only at the minimal-information operating point, not detection performance.\\n\"\n174\t  },\n175\t  {\n176\t    \"name\": \"Multiscale lzm Statistic\",\n177\t    \"description\": \"跨 benchmark 聚合的标准化 lz* 总分 (Hong, Lin & Cheng, 2021)。 PSN-IRT 12 模型 + 控制实验模型验证。\\n\",\n178\t    \"results\": {\n179\t      \"psn_12_models_lzm_flagged\": \"11/12\",\n180\t      \"psn_lzm_vs_individual_lz_agreement\": 0.92,\n181\t      \"ctrl_contam_lzm_d_15\": -2.8,\n182\t      \"ctrl_contam_lzm_d_50\": -0.8,\n183\t      \"lzm_adds_discrimination_beyond_individual\": false,\n184\t      \"lzm_reduces_false_positive\": false,\n185\t      \"cross_benchmark_correlation\": 0.15\n186\t    },\n187\t    \"tables\": [\n188\t      \"Table: lzm vs individual benchmark lz* — flagging agreement rate\"\n189\t    ],\n190\t    \"figures\": [],\n191\t    \"analysis\": \"lzm is a methodological contribution (implementing Hong et al. 2021 for LLM context) but does not improve detection in practice. Brief mention in Method section; not a headline result. Could become useful if applied to many small benchmarks where individual power is insufficient.\\n\\n\\nlzm aggregates standardized lz* scores across benchmarks. Since individual benchmark lz* values are already extreme for nearly all models (57/60 flagged), aggregation cannot reduce flagging — it merely averages universally-extreme signals. The cross-benchmark correlation of lz* is low (~0.15, only GSM8K-HumanEval significant at r=0.62), meaning lzm doesn't capture a coherent \\\"cross-benchmark contamination\\\" signal — it just averages independent benchmark-specific misfit. For ctrl-contam models, lzm Cohen's d (~2.8) is slightly lower than the best individual benchmark (MMLU d~3.1) because GSM8K and HellaSwag dilute the signal. lzm does not add value beyond individual benchmark analysis in this context. The theoretical appeal (Hong et al. 2021 designed lzm for multi-test batteries where aggregation recovers signal from individually-weak tests) doesn't apply because each benchmark already has strong model-level signal.\\n\"\n192\t  },\n193\t  {\n194\t    \"name\": \"Post-RL Hypothesis Test (GRPO)\",\n195\t    \"description\": \"Qwen3.5-9B: SFT contamination (15%) → GRPO training → measure if person-fit detection survives. Responds to Wang et al. (2025) MIA failure.\\n\",\n196\t    \"results\": {\n197\t      \"sft_only_lz_star_d\": -3.1,\n198\t      \"post_grpo_lz_star_d\": -0.25,\n199\t      \"post_grpo_ht_d\": -0.2,\n200\t      \"post_grpo_mia_auroc\": 0.51,\n201\t      \"post_grpo_mink_auroc\": 0.52,\n202\t      \"post_grpo_ppl_auroc\": 0.49,\n203\t      \"post_grpo_acc_delta\": 0.03,\n204\t      \"post_grpo_lz_star_value\": 8.1,\n205\t      \"post_grpo_clean_lz_star\": 8.65,\n206\t      \"post_grpo_delta_lz\": -0.55,\n207\t      \"memorization_fraction_post_rl\": 0.05,\n208\t      \"seen_unseen_acc_gap_post_rl\": 0.01,\n209\t      \"difficulty_ordering_restored\": true\n210\t    },\n211\t    \"tables\": [\n212\t      \"Table: Pre-RL vs Post-RL detection — all methods, showing collapse from d>2.5 to d<0.5\",\n213\t      \"Table: Seen/unseen accuracy gap pre-RL vs post-RL\"\n214\t    ],\n215\t    \"figures\": [],\n216\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the worst-tier defining result. ALL binary-only methods (lz*, HT, Guttman, U3, accuracy) and ALL token-probability methods (MIA, Min-K++, PPL) fail post-RL. The paper must honestly report this as a scope boundary: IRT-FIT detects SFT contamination but NOT post-RL contamination. Frame as contribution: \\\"We map the detection frontier — establishing where binary detection works (SFT) and precisely where it fails (post-RL).\\\" The RL boundary finding is itself publishable: it extends Wang et al. (2025) from 10 log-prob methods to include person-fit and all binary-only methods.\\n\\n\\nGRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) trains the model to maximize reward on a general objective. During RL, the model's policy is updated to produce correct answers through reasoning rather than memorization. The SFT-induced memorization signature — where specific items are answered correctly regardless of difficulty ordering — is overwritten because: (1) RL reward is based on correctness, not memorization; the model learns to derive answers rather than recall them; (2) GRPO uses group-relative rewards, normalizing performance within batches, which smooths out item-specific memorization spikes; (3) RL training updates ALL model parameters (not just LoRA adapters), distributing the contamination knowledge across the full parameter space and destroying the localized aberrance pattern. Post-RL, the model still achieves ~3% accuracy gain on seen items (residual knowledge) but the difficulty-ordering is restored — it gets hard-seen items wrong at expected rates. This makes lz* return to near-baseline (~8.1 vs clean 8.65). The seen/unseen accuracy gap collapses from ~0.10 (SFT) to ~0.01 (post-RL). HT also fails (d~0.20) because difficulty ordering is restored. MIA/Min-K++ fail (AUROC~0.51) consistent with Wang et al. (2025). ALL binary-only AND token-probability methods are at random. This is the structural limitation: RL transforms memorization into generalization-like behavior that is indistinguishable from genuine learning at all currently-accessible observation levels.\\n\"\n217\t  },\n218\t  {\n219\t    \"name\": \"Extra Benchmarks (BBH + MATH)\",\n220\t    \"description\": \"BBH (6,511 items) 和 MATH (5,000 items) 填充 1K-10K items 的 power gap。\\n\",\n221\t    \"results\": {\n222\t      \"bbh_available_in_psnirt\": false,\n223\t      \"math_available_in_psnirt\": false,\n224\t      \"bbh_4pl_self_calibrated\": true,\n225\t      \"math_4pl_self_calibrated\": true,\n226\t      \"bbh_power_15pct_sim\": 0.78,\n227\t      \"math_power_15pct_sim\": 0.68,\n228\t      \"bbh_ctrl_contam_d_15\": -1.8,\n229\t      \"math_ctrl_contam_d_15\": -1.4,\n230\t      \"bbh_ht_d_15\": -1.7,\n231\t      \"math_ht_d_15\": -1.3,\n232\t      \"power_function_fit_with_bbh_math\": 0.62,\n233\t      \"self_calibrated_vs_psnirt_degradation\": \"15-25% power loss\"\n234\t    },\n235\t    \"tables\": [\n236\t      \"Table: Power comparison — MMLU/HellaSwag/GSM8K (PSN-IRT) vs BBH/MATH (self-calibrated)\"\n237\t    ],\n238\t    \"figures\": [],\n239\t    \"analysis\": \"BBH and MATH fill the 1K-10K gap but with degraded performance due to self-calibration. This reinforces the PSN-IRT parameter dependency — IRT-FIT's power depends on parameter quality, not just benchmark size. Paper should report these as secondary results with the self-calibration caveat. Does not change worst-tier narrative.\\n\\n\\nBBH and MATH are unlikely to be in PSN-IRT (which covers standard benchmarks: MMLU, HellaSwag, GSM8K, ARC-C, HumanEval). Self-calibrating 4PL parameters from the limited LLM sample (N=12-20 models) introduces the same discrimination failure as Path D (a_r < 0.2), resulting in 15-25% power loss vs PSN-IRT calibrated parameters. BBH (6511 items) achieves simulated power ~0.78 at 15% but real-data d ~1.8 (lower than MMLU's ~3.1) due to self-calibration degradation and BBH's complex format (multi-step reasoning items where SFT contamination signal is weaker — the model may memorize the answer but not the reasoning path, and BBH scoring often requires the full reasoning). MATH (5000 items) is worse: math problems have high guessing variance and the SFT signal is diluted by the symbolic nature of responses. Both benchmarks confirm the power function trend (more items = more power) but with lower coefficients than MMLU-calibrated parameters would predict. R² drops from 0.957 to ~0.62 when self-calibrated benchmarks are included, showing the power function is parameter-dependent.\\n\"\n240\t  },\n241\t  {\n242\t    \"name\": \"MMLU Domain-Level Analysis\",\n243\t    \"description\": \"MMLU domain 级别分析：按 subject category 分别计算 person-fit， 识别 contamination 是全面还是集中在特定 domain。\\n\",\n244\t    \"results\": {\n245\t      \"gemini_subjects_flagged\": \"50/57\",\n246\t      \"gemini_all_domains_uniform\": true,\n247\t      \"gemini_stem_lz\": 21.0,\n248\t      \"gemini_humanities_lz\": 18.0,\n249\t      \"gemini_social_sciences_lz\": 16.0,\n250\t      \"gemini_other_lz\": 15.5,\n251\t      \"doubao_domain_asymmetric\": true,\n252\t      \"doubao_stem_lz\": -2.0,\n253\t      \"doubao_humanities_lz\": -13.0,\n254\t      \"yi_lightning_highest_lz\": 48.0,\n255\t      \"domain_concentration_index\": 0.85,\n256\t      \"contamination_uniform_pct\": \"80% of models\"\n257\t    },\n258\t    \"tables\": [\n259\t      \"Table: lz* by domain category for top-5 most anomalous models\",\n260\t      \"Table: Domain concentration index — fraction of models with uniform vs concentrated misfit\"\n261\t    ],\n262\t    \"figures\": [],\n263\t    \"analysis\": \"Domain analysis provides interpretability (which subjects drive anomalies) but doesn't improve detection power. 80% uniform contamination is unsurprising. Doubao's asymmetry is a case study for Discussion. Paper Section 5c (empirical findings). Under worst tier, this is a descriptive contribution, not a detection advance.\\n\\nGemini-1.5 with 100% MMLU accuracy trivially flags all domains — with perfect accuracy, every subject produces extreme positive lz* (over-consistent). STEM slightly higher than humanities because STEM items have lower average d parameter (ceiling effects more pronounced), amplifying the over-consistency signal. Most models (~80%) show uniform contamination across domains because: (1) if contamination is via training data inclusion, it's unlikely to be domain-selective (entire benchmark is either included or not); (2) SFT on randomly-sampled benchmark items distributes contamination proportionally across domains. Doubao shows domain asymmetry (STEM normal, humanities aberrant) suggesting selective knowledge — possibly stronger humanities training data or benchmark-specific tuning. This is interesting but doesn't affect the detection narrative. Yi-Lightning's extreme lz*=48 is driven by very high accuracy on hard items across all domains.\\n\"\n264\t  }\n265\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ba4edb1-d36b-4522-8fe1-2e7b2b802323","uuid":"8cfe53a6-57f9-4cb4-b3e0-688129bba6b6","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:03:26.458Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122444/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Controlled Contamination Pilot (Qwen × MMLU)\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct × 4 conditions (0%-seed1/0%-seed2/15%/50%) × MMLU. Go/no-go signal for the paper. Measures Δlz*, clean noise floor, memorization fraction.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_clean_seeds\": 2,\n      \"clean_seed1_lz_star\": 8.9,\n      \"clean_seed2_lz_star\": 8.4,\n      \"clean_vs_clean_delta_lz\": 0.5,\n      \"clean_mean_lz_star\": 8.65,\n      \"clean_std_lz_star\": 0.35,\n      \"contam_15_lz_star\": 4.1,\n      \"contam_50_lz_star\": 7.5,\n      \"delta_lz_contam15\": -4.55,\n      \"delta_lz_contam50\": -1.15,\n      \"cohens_d_contam15\": -3.1,\n      \"cohens_d_contam50\": -0.9,\n      \"theta_shift_15\": 0.28,\n      \"theta_shift_50\": 0.45,\n      \"memorization_fraction_15\": 0.36,\n      \"memorization_fraction_50\": 0.3,\n      \"theta_fixed_contam15_lz\": 11.5,\n      \"theta_fixed_contam50_lz\": 17.8,\n      \"theta_fixed_ratio\": 3.0,\n      \"ht_d_contam15\": -3.0,\n      \"ht_d_contam50\": -0.85,\n      \"acc_contam15\": 0.71,\n      \"acc_contam50\": 0.725,\n      \"acc_seen_contam15\": 0.78,\n      \"acc_unseen_contam15\": 0.68\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Pilot results — lz* and HT Cohen's d across conditions, with θ-free and θ-fixed\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Go signal confirmed for SFT scope. But HT matching lz* is a warning: the simpler statistic captures the same information at model level. Non-monotonicity under free-θ motivates θ-fixed methodology. Paper can claim SFT detection with |d|>2.5 but must acknowledge HT parity.\\n\\n\\nSFT with QLoRA directly injects benchmark items into training. Model memorizes ~36% of seen items (consistent with QLoRA's partial parameter update — full FT would memorize more). At 15%, 2106 items are seen; ~758 are memorized, creating a diffuse aberrance signal detectable at model level. Cohen's d ~3.1 because lz* aggregates 14042 item-level weak signals. Non-monotonicity at 50% (|d|=0.9 < 3.1) because θ-shift absorbs aberrance: contaminating 50% raises θ_MLE by ~0.45, making previously-aberrant correct answers look expected. θ-fixed analysis restores monotonicity (ratio ~3.0). HT matches lz* (d ~3.0 vs 3.1) because the dominant signal is difficulty-ordering violation — items the model \\\"should\\\" get wrong are answered correctly — and HT captures this with only item difficulty. The full 4PL parameterization (a, c, d) adds negligible incremental signal at model level when the difficulty violation is already large. Clean-vs-clean Δlz* ~0.5 confirms noise floor is small relative to contam signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Full Controlled Contamination (4 Models × 6 Levels × 3 Benchmarks)\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Gemma-4-31B, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3 × 0%/5%/10%/15%/25%/50% × MMLU/GSM8K/HellaSwag.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_families_validated\": 2,\n      \"qwen_mmlu_d15\": -3.1,\n      \"qwen_mmlu_d50\": -0.9,\n      \"llama_mmlu_d15\": -2.7,\n      \"llama_mmlu_d50\": -3.0,\n      \"mistral_mmlu_d15\": -2.4,\n      \"mistral_mmlu_d50\": -2.8,\n      \"gemma_mmlu_d15\": -1.8,\n      \"gemma_mmlu_d50\": -2.2,\n      \"qwen_gsm8k_d15\": -1.4,\n      \"llama_gsm8k_d15\": -1.2,\n      \"qwen_hellaswag_d15\": -1.8,\n      \"llama_hellaswag_d15\": -1.5,\n      \"qwen_ht_mmlu_d15\": -3.0,\n      \"llama_ht_mmlu_d15\": -2.6,\n      \"cross_family_mf_range\": \"0.30-0.40\",\n      \"cross_family_mf_std\": 0.04,\n      \"theta_fixed_monotonic_all\": true,\n      \"dose_response_5pct_detectable\": false,\n      \"dose_response_10pct_detectable\": \"marginal (d ~0.8-1.2 on MMLU only)\",\n      \"min_contam_for_detection_mmlu\": \"10-15%\",\n      \"min_contam_for_detection_gsm8k\": \"25%\",\n      \"min_contam_for_detection_hellaswag\": \"15-25%\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Cohen's d matrix — rows=models, cols=benchmarks×levels, bold best per column\",\n      \"Table: HT vs lz* Cohen's d comparison — no significant difference in any cell\",\n      \"Table: Memorization fraction across models and levels — 30-40% range\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SFT detection validated across 2 families with multiple seeds, but cross-family scope is limited. Gemma's weaker signal warns that larger models may approach the detection boundary even for SFT. GSM8K near the detectability threshold at 15%. HT parity across all 4×6×3 conditions eliminates empirical lz* advantage — the paper must rely on data-independent differentiation (parametric null, Snijders correction, 4PL item weighting).\\n\\n\\nOnly 2 families (Qwen + Llama) are fully validated with 3+ clean seeds each — Mistral and Gemma have single clean seeds, insufficient for rigorous Cohen's d with CI. Gemma-4-31B shows weaker detection (d ~1.8) because larger models have more latent capacity to absorb contamination without creating aberrant patterns — the 31B model generalizes from seen items more smoothly than 7-9B models, reducing the memorization fraction to ~0.25. GSM8K (1319 items) and HellaSwag (10042 items) show weaker detection than MMLU (14042 items), consistent with power function — fewer items = less aggregation power. GSM8K is particularly weak because its chain-of-thought format means SFT contamination partially teaches reasoning, not just memorization. HT matches lz* across all conditions (|d_HT - d_lz*| < 0.3) because difficulty-ordering violation dominates in all model-benchmark combinations. At 5% contamination, signal is indistinguishable from noise (only ~700 items seen on MMLU, ~250 memorized — insufficient for model-level detection). Cross-family MF consistency (0.30-0.40, std ~0.04) supports the generalizability of the memorization fraction concept. θ-fixed universally restores monotonicity, confirming this as a model-independent phenomenon.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Leaderboard Audit (ABANDONED)\",\n    \"description\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard v1 大规模扫描。已放弃因 IRT refit 失败。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"ABANDONED\",\n      \"reason\": \"a_r=0.111 (discrimination) + item alignment 54.56% → 13K audit infeasible\",\n      \"v1_data_availability\": \"13K+ models with per-item binary data\",\n      \"future_work_note\": \"Data availability mentioned in Discussion; audit deferred to future work pending neural IRT estimation advances\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ABANDONED. Not a paper weakness — frame as honest methodological caveat in Section 6 (IRT parameter estimation challenges). The 13K+ data availability is a future-work contribution. Paper uses PSN-IRT validated parameters (external calibration) instead.\\n\\n\\nClassical IRT estimation (MML-EM) with N=63-178 models produces a_r=0.111 (well below 0.2 threshold), making the refitted parameters effectively discrimination-free. lz* computed with these parameters collapses to an accuracy-like metric, losing the IRT advantage. The root cause is that classical IRT assumes large samples of independent examinees, but LLM response patterns violate this (models are not independent draws from a population; they share training data and architectures). Item alignment at 54.56% further degrades parameter quality. This failure is a genuine methodological finding about the gap between classical and neural IRT estimation requirements.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Natural Experiment (Base→Chat Pairs)\",\n    \"description\": \"v1 leaderboard base→chat model pairs: Δlz* = lz*(chat) - lz*(base) as SFT effect proxy.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_viable_pairs\": 5,\n      \"hellaswag_mean_deviation\": -3.2,\n      \"hellaswag_p_value\": 0.003,\n      \"gsm8k_mean_deviation\": 3.5,\n      \"gsm8k_p_value\": 0.015,\n      \"mmlu_p_value\": 0.18,\n      \"arcc_p_value\": 0.42,\n      \"individual_outliers_detected\": 0,\n      \"statistical_power_for_individual_detection\": \"insufficient\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Paired t-test results by benchmark — HellaSwag and GSM8K significant, MMLU/ARC-C not\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Deprioritized. Systematic SFT effects are a minor observation for Discussion section. No contamination detection capability. Frame as: \\\"natural experiments show SFT consistently changes lz* patterns, motivating the controlled contamination approach.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWith a_r=0.111, lz* is essentially accuracy-weighted, not IRT-weighted. The HellaSwag and GSM8K systematic effects reflect aggregate SFT training dynamics (SFT systematically changes performance patterns on these benchmarks), not contamination-specific signals. Only 5 viable pairs provide no statistical power for detecting individual contamination outliers — even a genuinely contaminated model would not stand out from the systematic SFT effect. MMLU and ARC-C show no significant systematic effect (p>0.1), possibly because SFT effects on these benchmarks are more heterogeneous across model families. The P2-optional priority is justified: results are mildly interesting (systematic SFT effects exist) but contribute nothing to the contamination detection narrative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"v1 IRT Refit Expanded (Path D Closure)\",\n    \"description\": \"扩大 v1 IRT 重拟合至 N≥500, 验证 a_r=0.111 是否因样本量不足。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_downloaded\": 500,\n      \"3pl_a_pearson_r\": 0.13,\n      \"2pl_a_pearson_r\": -0.02,\n      \"3pl_b_pearson_r\": 0.66,\n      \"a_r_threshold\": 0.2,\n      \"a_r_verdict\": \"dead (a_r < 0.2)\",\n      \"path_d_status\": \"closed\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: IRT refit metrics at N=63, N=178, N=500 — showing a_r plateau below 0.2\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Path D confirmed dead. Paper Section 6 (Empirical Findings) reports this as a methodological caveat: classical IRT estimation from heterogeneous LLM samples fails on discrimination. Frames the gap between classical (small N, homogeneous) and neural IRT (large N, heterogeneous) estimation as an open problem for the community.\\n\\n\\nExpanding from N=63→178 only improved a_r from 0.111 to 0.127. Extrapolating to N=500, the marginal gain is diminishing (~0.13-0.14). The fundamental issue is not sample size but model heterogeneity: LLMs from different families/scales violate the classical IRT assumption that all examinees are drawn from a single latent trait distribution. A 7B model and a 70B model are not \\\"two examinees differing in ability\\\" — they differ structurally in how they process items. Classical MML-EM cannot recover meaningful discrimination parameters from this heterogeneous sample. b_r stabilizes at ~0.66 because difficulty is a more robust property (hard items are hard for all models), while discrimination is model-architecture-dependent. This definitively closes Path D.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"4PL vs 3PL Ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"4PL vs 3PL(d=1) 检测灵敏度比较。量化 d 参数对 FAR 和 power 的影响。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"far_4pl\": 0.05,\n      \"far_3pl\": \"0.05-0.16\",\n      \"far_2pl\": \"0.50-1.00\",\n      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_mmlu\": 0.25,\n      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_hellaswag\": 0.24,\n      \"mean_power_gain_4pl_vs_3pl_gsm8k\": 0.19,\n      \"pct_mmlu_items_d_lt_0.95\": 0.46,\n      \"max_power_gain_at_low_d\": 0.55,\n      \"real_data_correlation_4pl_3pl\": 0.23,\n      \"deepseek_delta_lz\": 340\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: FAR and power by parameterization (2PL/3PL/4PL) × benchmark\",\n      \"Table: Power gain stratified by d-parameter bucket\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Strong methodological contribution independent of worst-tier narrative. 4PL is essential for LLM contamination detection — this justifies the PSN-IRT parameter dependency. However, since HT (difficulty-only) matches lz* empirically, the 4PL advantage is theoretical (better calibrated null, correct ceiling modeling) rather than empirical detection superiority. Tension: 4PL matters for calibration, but HT doesn't need 4PL and still matches.\\n\\n\\n4PL d parameter models the probability ceiling — P(correct) for very high ability examinees. 46% of MMLU items have d<0.95, meaning even the best models have <95% ceiling probability. When d=1 is forced (3PL), these items' response probabilities approach 1 for high-ability models, making any incorrect answer look extremely aberrant and inflating lz*. This creates both inflated FAR (false aberrance from normal ceiling effects) and reduced detection power (genuine contamination signal is masked by the d-induced noise). The +25pp mean power gain on MMLU reflects 4PL's ability to correctly model ceiling effects so that only genuine difficulty-ordering violations are flagged. 2PL is catastrophic because removing both c (guessing) and d (ceiling) makes the model unable to account for 40-60% of MMLU response variability. This result is tier-independent — it reflects a structural property of IRT parameterization, not a property of the detection frontier.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Baseline Comparison (≥5 Baselines)\",\n    \"description\": \"IRT-FIT vs 5+ baselines on ground-truth contaminated models: ConStat (re-test), Min-K%++ (token probs), accuracy z-score, PPL-based, HT, Guttman/U3, random.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_baselines\": 7,\n      \"n_baselines_beaten\": 3,\n      \"model_level_lz_star_d_15\": -3.1,\n      \"model_level_ht_d_15\": -3.0,\n      \"model_level_guttman_d_15\": -0.4,\n      \"model_level_u3_d_15\": -0.45,\n      \"model_level_acc_zscore\": 155,\n      \"constat_retest_auroc\": 0.88,\n      \"mink_sft_auroc\": 0.68,\n      \"ppl_sft_auroc\": 0.63,\n      \"random_auroc\": 0.5,\n      \"item_level_all_auroc_range\": \"0.50-0.63\",\n      \"item_level_best_oracle_rf\": 0.63,\n      \"lz_star_vs_ht_diff\": \"not significant (p>0.3)\",\n      \"bootstrap_ci_width_lz_d\": 1.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Model-level detection comparison — rows=methods (8), cols=models×conditions, Cohen's d or AUROC\",\n      \"Table: Item-level AUROC — all methods 0.50-0.63, confirming model-level aggregation is key\",\n      \"Table: Information access hierarchy — method, info required, SFT detection, post-RL detection\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Paper cannot claim detection superiority. Must reframe as: \\\"We establish the minimal-information operating point. Methods with more information access outperform — this is expected and honest. Our contribution is the framework (connecting psychometrics to LLM evaluation) and the characterization (θ-shift, memorization fraction, RL boundary). lz* beats only classical person-fit (Guttman/U3) and random — the 8× gap over Guttman shows IRT parameterization matters, but HT achieves the same with only difficulty.\\\" Bootstrap CIs (6/8 peer papers report none) remain a differentiator.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, lz* only beats 3 baselines: Guttman (d~0.4), U3 (d~0.45), and random (d=0). HT matches lz* (d~3.0 vs 3.1, difference not significant with n=3 clean seeds). ConStat with rephrased re-testing achieves AUROC 0.88 because re-testing provides a fundamentally richer signal — it creates a second independent measurement of the same items, enabling paired comparison. This is strictly more informative than binary-only. Min-K++ achieves SFT AUROC 0.68 because token-level probability distributions contain granular memorization signatures (specific logit patterns on memorized items) that binary responses discard. PPL-based detection gets 0.63 on SFT (above random but weak). All item-level methods are near-random (0.50-0.63) including oracle RF, confirming that contamination signal is diffuse — memorization fraction ~35% means 65% of seen items show no memorization signal. acc z-score achieves z~155 (trivially detects) but is non-specific: it can't distinguish contamination from genuine ability. The key worst-tier finding: methods with more information access (ConStat > Min-K++ > PPL > lz*) consistently outperform. lz*'s value is only at the minimal-information operating point, not detection performance.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multiscale lzm Statistic\",\n    \"description\": \"跨 benchmark 聚合的标准化 lz* 总分 (Hong, Lin & Cheng, 2021)。 PSN-IRT 12 模型 + 控制实验模型验证。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"psn_12_models_lzm_flagged\": \"11/12\",\n      \"psn_lzm_vs_individual_lz_agreement\": 0.92,\n      \"ctrl_contam_lzm_d_15\": -2.8,\n      \"ctrl_contam_lzm_d_50\": -0.8,\n      \"lzm_adds_discrimination_beyond_individual\": false,\n      \"lzm_reduces_false_positive\": false,\n      \"cross_benchmark_correlation\": 0.15\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: lzm vs individual benchmark lz* — flagging agreement rate\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"lzm is a methodological contribution (implementing Hong et al. 2021 for LLM context) but does not improve detection in practice. Brief mention in Method section; not a headline result. Could become useful if applied to many small benchmarks where individual power is insufficient.\\n\\n\\nlzm aggregates standardized lz* scores across benchmarks. Since individual benchmark lz* values are already extreme for nearly all models (57/60 flagged), aggregation cannot reduce flagging — it merely averages universally-extreme signals. The cross-benchmark correlation of lz* is low (~0.15, only GSM8K-HumanEval significant at r=0.62), meaning lzm doesn't capture a coherent \\\"cross-benchmark contamination\\\" signal — it just averages independent benchmark-specific misfit. For ctrl-contam models, lzm Cohen's d (~2.8) is slightly lower than the best individual benchmark (MMLU d~3.1) because GSM8K and HellaSwag dilute the signal. lzm does not add value beyond individual benchmark analysis in this context. The theoretical appeal (Hong et al. 2021 designed lzm for multi-test batteries where aggregation recovers signal from individually-weak tests) doesn't apply because each benchmark already has strong model-level signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Post-RL Hypothesis Test (GRPO)\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen3.5-9B: SFT contamination (15%) → GRPO training → measure if person-fit detection survives. Responds to Wang et al. (2025) MIA failure.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_only_lz_star_d\": -3.1,\n      \"post_grpo_lz_star_d\": -0.25,\n      \"post_grpo_ht_d\": -0.2,\n      \"post_grpo_mia_auroc\": 0.51,\n      \"post_grpo_mink_auroc\": 0.52,\n      \"post_grpo_ppl_auroc\": 0.49,\n      \"post_grpo_acc_delta\": 0.03,\n      \"post_grpo_lz_star_value\": 8.1,\n      \"post_grpo_clean_lz_star\": 8.65,\n      \"post_grpo_delta_lz\": -0.55,\n      \"memorization_fraction_post_rl\": 0.05,\n      \"seen_unseen_acc_gap_post_rl\": 0.01,\n      \"difficulty_ordering_restored\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Pre-RL vs Post-RL detection — all methods, showing collapse from d>2.5 to d<0.5\",\n      \"Table: Seen/unseen accuracy gap pre-RL vs post-RL\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the worst-tier defining result. ALL binary-only methods (lz*, HT, Guttman, U3, accuracy) and ALL token-probability methods (MIA, Min-K++, PPL) fail post-RL. The paper must honestly report this as a scope boundary: IRT-FIT detects SFT contamination but NOT post-RL contamination. Frame as contribution: \\\"We map the detection frontier — establishing where binary detection works (SFT) and precisely where it fails (post-RL).\\\" The RL boundary finding is itself publishable: it extends Wang et al. (2025) from 10 log-prob methods to include person-fit and all binary-only methods.\\n\\n\\nGRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) trains the model to maximize reward on a general objective. During RL, the model's policy is updated to produce correct answers through reasoning rather than memorization. The SFT-induced memorization signature — where specific items are answered correctly regardless of difficulty ordering — is overwritten because: (1) RL reward is based on correctness, not memorization; the model learns to derive answers rather than recall them; (2) GRPO uses group-relative rewards, normalizing performance within batches, which smooths out item-specific memorization spikes; (3) RL training updates ALL model parameters (not just LoRA adapters), distributing the contamination knowledge across the full parameter space and destroying the localized aberrance pattern. Post-RL, the model still achieves ~3% accuracy gain on seen items (residual knowledge) but the difficulty-ordering is restored — it gets hard-seen items wrong at expected rates. This makes lz* return to near-baseline (~8.1 vs clean 8.65). The seen/unseen accuracy gap collapses from ~0.10 (SFT) to ~0.01 (post-RL). HT also fails (d~0.20) because difficulty ordering is restored. MIA/Min-K++ fail (AUROC~0.51) consistent with Wang et al. (2025). ALL binary-only AND token-probability methods are at random. This is the structural limitation: RL transforms memorization into generalization-like behavior that is indistinguishable from genuine learning at all currently-accessible observation levels.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Extra Benchmarks (BBH + MATH)\",\n    \"description\": \"BBH (6,511 items) 和 MATH (5,000 items) 填充 1K-10K items 的 power gap。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bbh_available_in_psnirt\": false,\n      \"math_available_in_psnirt\": false,\n      \"bbh_4pl_self_calibrated\": true,\n      \"math_4pl_self_calibrated\": true,\n      \"bbh_power_15pct_sim\": 0.78,\n      \"math_power_15pct_sim\": 0.68,\n      \"bbh_ctrl_contam_d_15\": -1.8,\n      \"math_ctrl_contam_d_15\": -1.4,\n      \"bbh_ht_d_15\": -1.7,\n      \"math_ht_d_15\": -1.3,\n      \"power_function_fit_with_bbh_math\": 0.62,\n      \"self_calibrated_vs_psnirt_degradation\": \"15-25% power loss\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Power comparison — MMLU/HellaSwag/GSM8K (PSN-IRT) vs BBH/MATH (self-calibrated)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"BBH and MATH fill the 1K-10K gap but with degraded performance due to self-calibration. This reinforces the PSN-IRT parameter dependency — IRT-FIT's power depends on parameter quality, not just benchmark size. Paper should report these as secondary results with the self-calibration caveat. Does not change worst-tier narrative.\\n\\n\\nBBH and MATH are unlikely to be in PSN-IRT (which covers standard benchmarks: MMLU, HellaSwag, GSM8K, ARC-C, HumanEval). Self-calibrating 4PL parameters from the limited LLM sample (N=12-20 models) introduces the same discrimination failure as Path D (a_r < 0.2), resulting in 15-25% power loss vs PSN-IRT calibrated parameters. BBH (6511 items) achieves simulated power ~0.78 at 15% but real-data d ~1.8 (lower than MMLU's ~3.1) due to self-calibration degradation and BBH's complex format (multi-step reasoning items where SFT contamination signal is weaker — the model may memorize the answer but not the reasoning path, and BBH scoring often requires the full reasoning). MATH (5000 items) is worse: math problems have high guessing variance and the SFT signal is diluted by the symbolic nature of responses. Both benchmarks confirm the power function trend (more items = more power) but with lower coefficients than MMLU-calibrated parameters would predict. R² drops from 0.957 to ~0.62 when self-calibrated benchmarks are included, showing the power function is parameter-dependent.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"MMLU Domain-Level Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"MMLU domain 级别分析：按 subject category 分别计算 person-fit， 识别 contamination 是全面还是集中在特定 domain。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gemini_subjects_flagged\": \"50/57\",\n      \"gemini_all_domains_uniform\": true,\n      \"gemini_stem_lz\": 21.0,\n      \"gemini_humanities_lz\": 18.0,\n      \"gemini_social_sciences_lz\": 16.0,\n      \"gemini_other_lz\": 15.5,\n      \"doubao_domain_asymmetric\": true,\n      \"doubao_stem_lz\": -2.0,\n      \"doubao_humanities_lz\": -13.0,\n      \"yi_lightning_highest_lz\": 48.0,\n      \"domain_concentration_index\": 0.85,\n      \"contamination_uniform_pct\": \"80% of models\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: lz* by domain category for top-5 most anomalous models\",\n      \"Table: Domain concentration index — fraction of models with uniform vs concentrated misfit\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Domain analysis provides interpretability (which subjects drive anomalies) but doesn't improve detection power. 80% uniform contamination is unsurprising. Doubao's asymmetry is a case study for Discussion. Paper Section 5c (empirical findings). Under worst tier, this is a descriptive contribution, not a detection advance.\\n\\nGemini-1.5 with 100% MMLU accuracy trivially flags all domains — with perfect accuracy, every subject produces extreme positive lz* (over-consistent). STEM slightly higher than humanities because STEM items have lower average d parameter (ceiling effects more pronounced), amplifying the over-consistency signal. Most models (~80%) show uniform contamination across domains because: (1) if contamination is via training data inclusion, it's unlikely to be domain-selective (entire benchmark is either included or not); (2) SFT on randomly-sampled benchmark items distributes contamination proportionally across domains. Doubao shows domain asymmetry (STEM normal, humanities aberrant) suggesting selective knowledge — possibly stronger humanities training data or benchmark-specific tuning. This is interesting but doesn't affect the detection narrative. Yi-Lightning's extreme lz*=48 is driven by very high accuracy on hard items across all domains.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
